      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks but i dont actually need the lambda to read the logs, it just needs to dump them there. it's a write-only thing. so s3:GetObject isnt needed. \n\ni checked the bucket policy too and it looks fine. is there anything else that could be blocking it? maybe something in the execution role or the way the s3 resource is defined?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I see where the confusion lies. If your Lambda function is strictly for writing logs and not reading them, then indeed, the \"s3:GetObject\" action is not necessary. It sounds like the issue might be elsewhere, perhaps in the way the S3 resource is defined or in the execution role of your Lambda function.\n\nLet's take a closer look at the execution role. The role should have the necessary permissions to write logs to your S3 bucket. Here's a basic example of what the policy attached to your Lambda execution role might look like:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nMake sure that the execution role has the \"s3:PutObject\" permission as shown above. If the role doesn't have this permission, your Lambda function won't be able to write logs to the S3 bucket.\n\nIf the execution role's permissions are correct, then the issue might be with how the S3 resource is defined in your Lambda",
      "source": "target",
